  "body": "From the re-review of PR #65 (#62). The axis fit is a plain least-squares fit, so a keyway, a single radial hole or another off-centre feature drags the fitted origin: a 72-segment D20 shaft sitting exactly on G54 fits 0.066 mm off with a 5 mm keyway and 0.13 mm off with a 10 mm one - far over the 0.0005 mm spindle tolerance. The part is refused (safe - before #62 the generator silently turned about the biased axis), but the message tells the user to align the part's axis and move the work offset, which cannot help.\n\nAlso: no motion-crate test pins axis_refine's no-worse guard on a non-revolved part (keyed/flatted revolve must keep the eigen direction exactly) - the case most likely to drift for rotary ops.\n\nDone means: an outlier-resistant axis fit (e.g. fit on the facets whose normal lines meet the candidate axis, then re-fit) so a correctly placed keyed shaft passes; or at least a refusal that tells \"fit biased by off-centre features\" apart from \"part misplaced\"; plus the keyed-revolve guard test in crates/motion.",
